    origin: "Erik Hollnagel, Robert Wears and Jeffrey Braithwaite set out Safety-II in a 2015 white paper published with EUROCONTROL, building on resilience engineering work from the 2000s. Safety-I defines safety as the absence of failure and learns only from the rare failures; Safety-II defines it as the presence of successful outcomes under varying conditions and learns from the many cases that go right. Aviation and healthcare adopted it first; security operations has begun to.",
    structure: "The structure runs two learning loops side by side. The Safety-I loop continues: incidents are investigated and fixed. The Safety-II loop studies normal work: how analysts triage when alerts spike, how engineers deploy safely when the pipeline is degraded, how operators notice something odd before any alert fires. Methods include observing work as done (not as documented), debriefing successful handling of difficult days, and tracing the adjustments people made to keep the outcome good. Findings feed design: making the successful adjustment easier, giving it tooling, removing the obstacle that made it necessary, or protecting the slack that allowed it. Metrics shift from counting incidents to measuring capacity: how much variability the team absorbed, how often adjustments were needed, and where the margin ran thin. The two loops meet in the recognition that failures and successes come from the same performance variability.",
